Expanded the device data model (`#48 <https://github.com/sandialabs/PEAT/issues/48>`_) and upgraded the Elastic Common Schema (ECS) version PEAT follows:

- Upgraded the Elastic Common Schema (ECS) version PEAT follows from 8.10.0 to 9.5.0. The ECS ``event.type`` value ``device`` (added in ECS 9.2.0) is now accepted, ``process.pgid`` was removed from the ``peat-logs`` mapping (removed in ECS 9.0.0), and log records now use the ECS field ``process.parent.pid`` instead of ``process.parent.process.pid``. Host documents now carry ``event.kind: asset`` and the ECS 9.5.0 ``host.tags`` field, and documents in the events index have a single ``event.kind`` value.
- New device data fields: event ``reason``, ``code`` and ``log_level``; VLANs (``Interface.vlan``, ``Interface.vlans``, ``Interface.parent``, ``related.vlans``); cumulative network statistics per interface, service, device and protocol (``statistics``, ``protocol_statistics``); link redundancy (``Interface.redundancy``: PRP, HSR, RSTP, LAG, failover, ...); running processes (``processes``); PLC tasks (``logic.tasks``); routes (``routes``); the device clock and its time sources (``clock``); NAMUR NE 107 health (``health``) and key switch position (``key_switch``); front panel LEDs, displays, switches, sensors and status flags (``indicators``); installed software (``software``); service security facts such as encryption, anonymous access and accepted factory default credentials (``Service.security``; secrets are never stored); file categories (``File.category``); and an IEC 61850 configuration summary (``iec61850``). See the data model documentation for the canonical protocol names, relationship types and NE 107 mapping.
- Relationships between devices and other endpoints (``relationships``): communication peers with their roles (e.g. DNP3 master and outstation), ARP and socket-table neighbors, serial links, tunnels, log, DNS and time servers, route next hops, and IEC 61850 GOOSE, SV and report publications and subscriptions, each with the evidence it was found in (``config``, ``arp``, ``netstat``, ``log``, ``scl``, ...). A configured peer and its observed sessions are one relationship. Time sources and route gateways become relationships automatically. Relationships are exported to ``device-data-relationships.jsonl`` and the new ``ot-device-relationships`` Elasticsearch index (``ELASTIC_RELATIONSHIPS_INDEX``).
- Owner-supplied asset context (``asset``: criticality, owner, site, zone, Purdue level, tags, ...) can be set for a host in the ``hosts`` section of the configuration file and is exported as ``host.asset`` in every document.
- Baselining: new ``peat baseline-create``, ``peat baseline-compare`` and ``peat baseline-show`` commands and the ``peat.api.baseline_api`` API (``create_baseline``, ``compare_to_baseline``, ``compare_to_known_defaults``, ``load_baselines``, ``save_baselines``). Devices are compared against baselines made from previous pulls, engineer-provided golden configurations, or "typical device" profiles of several units (``--scope device_type``), and against the factory defaults and vendor hardening recommendations declared by PEAT modules (``--known-defaults``; SEL relays and RTACs, Sage, M340 and ControlLogix). Findings are written to ``baseline-findings.json``, ``baseline-summary.json`` and the new ``ot-device-baseline-findings`` index (``ELASTIC_BASELINE_FINDINGS_INDEX``). ``--fail-on SEVERITY`` exits with code 3 when a finding of at least that severity is found.
- Module developers can declare ``DeviceModule.known_defaults`` (baselines) and ``DeviceModule.factory_default_credentials``. ``DeviceModule.is_factory_default_credential()`` sets ``Service.security.default_credentials`` only when a factory default credential was accepted by the device.
- New ``IEC61850SCL`` parse module and ``peat.parsing.scl`` parser for IEC 61850 SCL files (SCD, ICD, IID, CID, SED, SSD): one device per IED with the ``iec61850`` configuration, interfaces, ``mms``/``goose``/``sv`` services, ``publishes_to``/``subscribes_to``/``reports_to`` relationships joined across devices by their control block ``reference``, and ``scl-validation`` alerts for duplicate or out-of-range APPIDs and invalid address values. ``TEMPLATE`` IEDs (ICD files) are named ``TEMPLATE@<file name>``; IEDs with a ``0.0.0.0``, default or shared IP address keep it in ``iec61850.access_points`` and are not merged with other devices. SEL relays also parse the ``SET_61850.CID`` file they download.
- SEL relays and RTACs: interface statistics, redundancy and physical ports (``ETH``), DNP3, Modbus, C37.118, MIRRORED BITS and RTAC client relationships, clock and SNTP sources, self-test indicators and NE 107 health, front panel LED definitions, ``iec61850`` from ``SET_61850.CID``, a file inventory with categories, RTAC task CPU usage, event reasons and trip alerts, login security (factory default credentials), and known-default baselines.
- Schneider Electric Sage: VxWorks tasks as processes, software (OpenSSL, IPCOM components, ISaGRAF), clock, routes, LED definitions, serial ports and their configured peers, a file inventory, event reason and action, factory default credential detection and known defaults. Modicon M340: Modbus, web and SMTP service statistics, LED indicators, module status, health, MAC and firmware, SNMP security and trap managers, NTP, factory default credential detection and known defaults.
- GE UR relays: ARP neighbors, routes, GDOI/OCSP/SCEP server dependencies, port redundancy, USB statistics and event reasons. GE D25 RTUs: IP/UDP/TCP protocol statistics and error codes. Siemens SIPROTEC: EN100 and optical channel interfaces with statistics and RSTP/PRP/HSR redundancy, GOOSE and SNMP statistics, SNTP sources, WebMonitor software, uptime and start time.
- Rockwell ControlLogix: CIP identity status decoded into indicators, key switch position and NE 107 health; network statistics, connections, ARP neighbors, routes and web clients from the communication module web pages; the FTP file listing in ``files``; factory default credential detection and known defaults. L5X files: tasks and I/O tree (scanner to adapter) relationships.
- Fortinet FortiGate: VLAN, aggregate and redundant interfaces, NIC statistics, processes, routes, NTP sources, ARP, IPsec, syslog, FortiAnalyzer, DNS, SMTP and SNMP manager relationships, and event reason, code and log level.
- Shared command parsers: interface statistics and VLAN/parent interfaces from ``ifconfig`` (now also the BusyBox and Linux formats) and ``/proc/net/dev``; relationships from ARP tables and ``netstat`` (VxWorks and Linux); new parsers for Linux ``netstat -tulnp`` and ``ip route``. iDirect: processes, listening services and routes. Camlin Totus: NTP time sources, device clock, OpenVPN and DNP3 relationships. OpenPLC Runtime v4: PLC tasks, the mode switch and EtherCAT statistics. SCEPTRE: client relationships, I/O provider dependency and the cycle task. Windows CE: processes, files and socket relationships. Woodward MicroNet: file inventory. The example module stores its connected IPs as relationships.
